A||||9||Uaana||3.4 Erratic Idle||||1||
Z||000000||Uaana||08-09-2001||02:02 AM||||I've seen mention that a surging idle on 3.4 swaps is a common thing. Well I was at the shop tonight and witnessed this first hand (The ECM is constantly hunting for the right mix)<BR>So 3.4 guys whats the fix? Every thing else seemed to check out. ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Joe Torma||08-09-2001||02:52 AM||||Here's a trick I used to help smooth 3.1 conversions...when I got my 3400pushrod, I noticed that there is a small hole drilled in the throttle plate.  So I tried drilling one on my 3.1 and I was able to back out the Idle screw and it idled better.  I don't think the engine got enough air at idle(without over doing the idle screw and idle air) with the stock TB.<P>I guess before you drill, make sure your O2 sensor is ok, and you have no vac leaks and check your Idle air motor.||||reg||1
Z||000002||ironhedno3||08-09-2001||12:19 PM||||kinda weird..mine idles like a 2.8..didnt at first but after a new iac,map sensor.and a gm ignition module it is fine..not that i was throwing parts at it but the iac wasnt working at all ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Alex4mula||08-09-2001||03:07 PM||||I have that problem in my 3.4. I installed a new IAC to take it out of the formula. MAP, O2 and Module are OK. I have looked many times for vacuum leaks and tested the lines. It will idle ok at the beginning, but If I run it, when I stop and start it back, then many times the idles goes erratic and sometimes dies when stopping completely. Sometimes it works OK. It is very annoying. Any help ins this area welcome.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Black88GT||08-09-2001||03:25 PM||||I've never had this problem.  Mine is a 5 speed though, I think 1 or more of you have auto 3.4s.  <P>Alex did you test the EGR system yet?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Joe Torma||08-09-2001||03:35 PM||||Are you all setting your base idle correctly?<P>-Ground out terminals in ADL connector<BR>-Put key in RUN<BR>-Let sit for a minute to allow the IAC to screw in.<BR>-Unplug IAC, turn off key<BR>-Unground ADL(???I forget, but pretty sure)<BR>-Start car and adjust the Idle screw to get it to run at like 500-600rpm.<BR>-Turn off car and plug IAC back in and you should be good to go.<P>If you can't get it to idle(like I couldn't with my 3.1 and rowdy cam) drill the hole.  Don't let any metal into the plenum of course.  Start with a VERY SMALL(like 1/16)drill bit, drill slowly...and see how it works.  That idle screw should only be screwed in about mid way somewhere or your TPS will be out of whack it needs to be about .54V.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Uaana||08-09-2001||08:27 PM||||Talked with my mechanic this morning.<BR>guess it sounds more like an unburnt fuel problem. (High hco levels) Possibly a bad dist.. keep you posted||||reg||1
Z||000007||Alex4mula||08-10-2001||09:01 AM||||Black; I did test the EGR system. Mine is 5 speed also (Formula).<P>Joe; Is that procedure in the service manual? Isn't 500-600RPM too low? TIA||||reg||1
Z||000008||Joe Torma||08-10-2001||11:18 AM||||Yes, that Idle sequence is in the manual somewhere...  It IS too low to run at, but the IAC takes care of the rest.  The computer needs to have a set amount of air coming in through the throttle plate, and that sequence sets that.<P>I couldn't get mine to idle without having the Idle screw cranked in till it stopped, which isn't good either.  Probably due to the cam I used(custom grind--@ the edge of computer toleration).<P>Set the idle...let us know what happens!<P>Joe||||reg||1
Z||000009||Alex4mula||08-10-2001||01:22 PM||||Thanks Joe. I'll try to do that today or tomorrow. I'll post results.||||reg||1
